-- title:
--   Cuspidality of the adelic weight-one lift
-- statement:
--   Let $n$ be a nonzero natural number and let $f:\mathfrak{H}\to\mathbb{C}$ be a function on the upper half-plane which is holomorphic (differentiable as a map of complex manifolds), satisfies $f\mid_1\varepsilon=f$ for every $\varepsilon\in\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ lying in $\Gamma_1(n)$ (weight-one slash action through the inclusion into $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$), obeys a global bound $\lVert f(\tau)\rVert\le C/(\operatorname{Im}\tau)^{1/2}$ for some real constant $C$ and all $\tau$, and is such that $f\mid_1\delta$ is zero at $i\infty$ for every $\delta\in\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$. Then the adelic lift `weightOneLift` of $f$ at the level ideal generated by $n$ in $\mathcal{O}_\mathbb{Q}$ is a cuspidal function in the sense of `IsCuspidalFn`: its constant term along the unipotent family $x\mapsto\begin{pmatrix}1&x\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$, integrated over the adele ring against the additive adelic Haar measure conditioned on the adelic box (archimedean part in the infinite box, finite part integral), vanishes at every point of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_\mathbb{Q})$. Here the lift sends $g$ to $(f\mid_1 h')(i)\cdot\det(h')$ for a chosen decomposition $g=\gamma\, h\, u$ with $\gamma$ rational, $h$ of trivial finite part and $h'=$ its real component in $\mathrm{GL}_2^+(\mathbb{R})$, and $u$ in the intersection of the level-$(n)$ subgroup with the kernel of the archimedean projection; $g$ admitting no such decomposition is sent to $0$.
--
--   This is the adelic counterpart of the classical fact that the function on $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_\mathbb{Q})$ attached to a weight-one cusp form of level $n$ is cuspidal, the vanishing of the unipotent constant term corresponding to the vanishing of the zeroth Fourier coefficient at the cusps. It feeds the construction of the automorphic realisation of dihedral weight-one forms, being used in the statements that a primitive form gives rise to a smooth cuspidal realisation whose values agree with the lift, and that this lift is nonzero with prescribed Hecke behaviour.
